  "query_preview": "..Similar to yesterday, a large upper low continues to \nslowly move into the Pacific NW, deepening with eastern extent. A \ntight southwest-northeast oriented pressure gradient remains in \nplace across nearly all height levels resulting in another afternoon \nof enhanced southwesterly flow overhead. With another clear and dry \nafternoon where daytime surface heating is favorable, elevated winds \nare forecast to mix to the surface yielding gusty conditions through \nmuch of the afternoon statewide. Most areas across UT and SW WY can \nonce again expect to see gusts in the 25-35mph range, though areas \nwest of I-15 may see gusts approach (and potentially exceed) 45mph \ngiven stronger winds aloft with higher surface temperatures to \nencourage stronger mixing. This general pattern is expected to \ncontinue into Sunday with enhanced afternoon southwesterlies \ndeveloping around the 10AM-12PM timeframe each day, though a slight \ndecrease in magnitude is expected Sunday given preceding cloud cover \nwhich may inhibit mixing for areas east of I-15.  \n\nCome Sunday, moisture associated with Hurricane Marie is forecast to \nphase into the mean upper flow as as the southern stream pushes \nnorth and increases in magnitude. PWAT anomalies on the order of 0.5-\n0.8 above seasonal normals (potentially exceeding 200% normal \nvalues) are forecast to push north across much of Utah with this \nincrease in southwesterly flow. Widespread ascent will overspread as \nthe strengthening southern stream phases into the mean flow with the \naforementioned upper low, with a quick eastern progression of the \nmain precipitation axis through the afternoon hours. As such, \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms are forecast to develop in the \nmorning hours across much of southern and central UT, potentially \nnudging into northern UT as well. Despite enhanced flow aloft \nsuggesting fast moving storms, robust moisture content within the \natmosphere continues to yield concern for flash flooding across \ncentral and southern UT, where a slight risk (level 2/4) for \nexcessive rainfall has been outlined by the Weather Prediction \nCenter (WPC). Current guidance suggests hourly rainfall rates \nranging from 0.4-0.6\"/hr, with some isolated rates nearing 0.6-\n0.8\"/hr rates possible. \n\nRegarding the severe threat, it appears to have become more isolated \nin nature given ample cloud cover from the morning hours into the \nafternoon resulting in a stable layer across much of the state. \nAmple shear exists on the order of 40-50kts, though only modest \nbuoyancy develops in the midst of persistent cloud cover and moist \nboundary layer with robust convection likely struggling to develop \nin the afternoon hours. If any stronger convection were to develop, \nthe most probable locations would be far northwest UT where \nfrontogenetic forcing is forecast to be strongest, and perhaps \nsoutheast UT where a mid-level impulse is forecast to traverse in \nthe afternoon hours, particularly along Castle Country. The main \nhazards will be isolated gusts to around 58mph and instances of sub-\nsevere hail less than 1\". \n\nCome Monday and Tuesday, the upper low progresses east leaving the \nforecast area in a dry westerly flow regime keeping conditions calm \nto start the week. On Wednesday, ensemble guidance has come to \nbetter agreement with roughly 50-60% of all ensembles supporting \nmoisture return in some capacity to UT. However, all members within \ncluster guidance support moisture return in some capacity through \nthe remainder of the week, resulting in timing being the only \nuncertainty with this forecast rather than *if* moisture will \nreturn.",
